1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
In 2013, Angelina Jolie said she carried a

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,920
mutation in a gene called B R C

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:07,120
A one.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:09,520
She'd inherited it, so it was in every

5
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:12,080
cell of her body, and her doctors estimated

6
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:15,520
her breast cancer risk at eighty-seven per cent.

7
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:17,920
But the same change can also happen later

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,480
in life, in a single breast cell, and

9
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:23,040
then no child can inherit it.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,093
So why does it matter which cell a

11
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:27,160
mutation is in, and which part of the

12
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:27,920
DNA?

13
00:00:29,134 --> 00:00:31,773
As far as mutations go, your body has

14
00:00:31,894 --> 00:00:33,533
two kinds of cells.

15
00:00:33,734 --> 00:00:37,053
Most are somatic cells, body cells like skin,

16
00:00:37,174 --> 00:00:38,653
muscle and liver.

17
00:00:38,734 --> 00:00:41,453
A few are germ-line cells, the cells that

18
00:00:41,534 --> 00:00:44,653
make eggs or sperm, and the gametes themselves.

19
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:48,666
Say a mutation happens in one skin cell.

20
00:00:48,907 --> 00:00:52,106
That cell divides by mitosis, both daughter cells

21
00:00:52,187 --> 00:00:54,066
copy the change, and so on.

22
00:00:54,347 --> 00:00:56,186
So it ends up in a patch, while

23
00:00:56,227 --> 00:00:58,146
the rest of the body stays normal.

24
00:00:58,427 --> 00:01:01,386
That's a somatic mutation, and the earlier it

25
00:01:01,427 --> 00:01:03,506
happens, the bigger the patch.

26
00:01:03,867 --> 00:01:05,706
If the change hits a gene that controls

27
00:01:05,787 --> 00:01:08,986
cell division, and more mutations follow, the patch

28
00:01:09,107 --> 00:01:11,866
can keep dividing, and become a cancer.

29
00:01:11,947 --> 00:01:14,906
That's how UV causes most skin cancers.

30
00:01:16,134 --> 00:01:18,573
A germ-line mutation is different.

31
00:01:18,854 --> 00:01:21,013
Suppose it's in a cell that makes sperm.

32
00:01:21,494 --> 00:01:24,453
A sperm carrying it fertilises an egg, so

33
00:01:24,534 --> 00:01:26,853
the zygote has it, and so does every

34
00:01:26,934 --> 00:01:28,853
cell copied from the zygote.

35
00:01:29,174 --> 00:01:31,893
That includes the new animal's own germ cells,

36
00:01:31,974 --> 00:01:33,973
so it can be passed on again, and

37
00:01:34,054 --> 00:01:35,733
enter the gene pool.

38
00:01:35,814 --> 00:01:38,533
The parent's body isn't affected, and the offspring

39
00:01:38,614 --> 00:01:40,533
may show nothing, if the new allele is

40
00:01:40,614 --> 00:01:41,333
recessive.

41
00:01:42,534 --> 00:01:44,093
So that's Jolie's case.

42
00:01:44,294 --> 00:01:47,493
Her inherited copy was in every cell, including

43
00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:50,373
those that make eggs, so each egg had

44
00:01:50,454 --> 00:01:52,613
a one in two chance of carrying it.

45
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:55,733
A somatic mutation is copied only into one

46
00:01:55,894 --> 00:01:58,853
cell's descendants, so it affects part of one

47
00:01:58,934 --> 00:02:01,413
organism, and isn't inherited.

48
00:02:01,494 --> 00:02:04,053
A germ-line mutation can reach every cell of

49
00:02:04,134 --> 00:02:05,573
the next generation.

50
00:02:05,654 --> 00:02:09,253
That's why, in animals, only germ-line mutations add

51
00:02:09,294 --> 00:02:10,293
to the gene pool.

52
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:14,700
Now the second question, where in the DNA?

53
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,180
Only about one to two per cent of

54
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:20,780
human DNA is coding DNA, the stretches whose

55
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:22,860
codons are translated into protein.

56
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,140
A change there alters a codon, so it

57
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:28,940
can be silent, swap an amino acid, add

58
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,340
an early stop, or shift the frame.

59
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,980
The rest is non-coding DNA, and much of

60
00:02:35,020 --> 00:02:36,860
it was once written off as junk.

61
00:02:38,067 --> 00:02:41,306
But some non-coding DNA controls genes.

62
00:02:41,587 --> 00:02:44,866
Remember the promoter, where RNA polymerase binds to

63
00:02:44,947 --> 00:02:45,746
copy a gene.

64
00:02:46,307 --> 00:02:48,626
Change a base there, and the polymerase may

65
00:02:48,707 --> 00:02:52,306
bind less well, so less mRNA is made,

66
00:02:52,387 --> 00:02:52,946
or none.

67
00:02:53,587 --> 00:02:56,066
Every codon is still correct, there's just less

68
00:02:56,147 --> 00:02:56,706
protein.

69
00:02:57,347 --> 00:03:01,026
Other control stretches, called regulatory sequences, can sit

70
00:03:01,107 --> 00:03:03,266
far away, and decide when a gene is

71
00:03:03,387 --> 00:03:05,586
switched on, and in which cells.

72
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:09,920
Most mammals stop making lactase, the enzyme that

73
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:12,640
digests milk sugar, after weaning.

74
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:16,000
But many adults from dairy-farming populations keep making

75
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:16,720
it.

76
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:18,720
In many of them, the cause is one

77
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:22,000
base change in a regulatory sequence, about fourteen

78
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:25,120
thousand bases before the lactase gene.

79
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,000
The enzyme is identical, the gene just stays

80
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:29,120
switched on.

81
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:31,920
It's inherited, and it helped milk drinkers, so

82
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:33,840
it spread through those populations.

83
00:03:35,067 --> 00:03:38,186
Non-coding DNA sits inside genes too.

84
00:03:38,507 --> 00:03:41,866
Many genes contain introns, stretches cut out of

85
00:03:41,907 --> 00:03:43,946
the mRNA before translation.

86
00:03:44,587 --> 00:03:46,186
A change in the middle of an intron

87
00:03:46,427 --> 00:03:47,546
usually does nothing.

88
00:03:48,107 --> 00:03:49,946
But a change at the signal that marks

89
00:03:50,027 --> 00:03:52,026
where to cut can make the cell cut

90
00:03:52,107 --> 00:03:54,906
in the wrong place, and the protein changes.

91
00:03:55,547 --> 00:03:58,826
And some non-coding DNA has no known job,

92
00:03:58,907 --> 00:04:00,986
so changes there are often neutral.

93
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:05,640
So which matters more, coding or non-coding?

94
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:07,480
Neither, as a rule.

95
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:10,120
A coding change can be silent, and a

96
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:12,520
non-coding change can switch a gene off.

97
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:15,640
What counts is whether the changed stretch does

98
00:04:15,720 --> 00:04:18,040
a job, and what the change does to

99
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:18,440
it.

100
00:04:19,667 --> 00:04:21,506
Put the two questions together.

101
00:04:21,827 --> 00:04:23,906
Where it is in the body decides who

102
00:04:23,947 --> 00:04:27,346
it can affect, one organism, or its descendants

103
00:04:27,427 --> 00:04:27,826
too.

104
00:04:28,387 --> 00:04:30,546
Where it is in the DNA decides what

105
00:04:30,627 --> 00:04:33,586
it does, change a protein, or change how

106
00:04:33,667 --> 00:04:34,746
much is made.

107
00:04:35,027 --> 00:04:37,186
A UV change in a skin cell's gene

108
00:04:37,267 --> 00:04:40,386
can start a cancer, while lactase persistence is

109
00:04:40,467 --> 00:04:43,186
a germ-line change in a regulatory sequence.

110
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:45,920
Two things to hold on to.

111
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:49,120
Somatic stays with you, germ line can go

112
00:04:49,160 --> 00:04:49,920
to your kids.

113
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:52,880
That's true for animals, but plants can make

114
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:55,680
flowers from body tissue that carries a mutation.

115
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:58,800
And non-coding doesn't mean unimportant.

116
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:00,800
Ask what the stretch does.

117
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,600
Two exam questions now.

118
00:05:04,080 --> 00:05:07,920
First, fruit fly larvae were exposed to X-rays.

119
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:11,120
In fly A, a mutation in one developing

120
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:13,280
eye cell left the adult with a white

121
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:15,360
patch in one red eye.

122
00:05:15,680 --> 00:05:18,080
In fly B, a mutation hit a cell

123
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:21,080
that makes sperm, and fly B looked normal.

124
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:24,640
Identify each type, then explain how their effects

125
00:05:24,720 --> 00:05:27,960
differ, on each fly and on its offspring.

126
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:29,520
Pause here to answer.

127
00:05:39,934 --> 00:05:41,693
Fly A's is somatic.

128
00:05:41,774 --> 00:05:45,533
Mitosis copied it into that eye cell's descendants,

129
00:05:45,654 --> 00:05:47,613
so only a patch changed.

130
00:05:47,694 --> 00:05:50,333
It isn't in A's gametes, so its offspring

131
00:05:50,454 --> 00:05:52,333
can't inherit the patch.

132
00:05:52,494 --> 00:05:54,653
Fly B's is germ-line.

133
00:05:54,694 --> 00:05:57,133
It didn't change fly B, but sperm from

134
00:05:57,214 --> 00:05:59,693
that cell carry it, so some offspring have

135
00:05:59,774 --> 00:06:01,853
it in every cell, and can pass it

136
00:06:01,974 --> 00:06:02,813
on.

137
00:06:02,854 --> 00:06:05,293
Saying fly A's offspring get the patch is

138
00:06:05,374 --> 00:06:06,733
the classic mistake.

139
00:06:07,934 --> 00:06:08,493
Second.

140
00:06:08,974 --> 00:06:12,253
Two mutations are found in an enzyme's gene.

141
00:06:12,334 --> 00:06:15,053
P is a substitution in the promoter, and

142
00:06:15,134 --> 00:06:16,973
cells with it make about a tenth as

143
00:06:17,054 --> 00:06:18,573
much mRNA.

144
00:06:18,774 --> 00:06:21,533
Q is a substitution in the coding sequence,

145
00:06:21,734 --> 00:06:24,173
changing the codon C U U to C

146
00:06:24,254 --> 00:06:25,213
U C.

147
00:06:25,534 --> 00:06:28,333
A student claims Q is more significant, because

148
00:06:28,374 --> 00:06:29,973
it's in coding DNA.

149
00:06:30,254 --> 00:06:31,413
Assess the claim.

150
00:06:31,694 --> 00:06:32,973
Pause here to answer.

151
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:45,080
The claim isn't supported.

152
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:48,040
C U U and C U C both

153
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:50,840
code for leucine, so Q keeps the same

154
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:51,920
amino acid.

155
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:54,600
P changes no codon, but it cuts the

156
00:06:54,680 --> 00:06:57,320
mRNA to about a tenth, so the cell

157
00:06:57,520 --> 00:06:59,480
likely makes much less enzyme.

158
00:06:59,720 --> 00:07:02,120
On the evidence given, P has the clearer

159
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:02,920
effect.

160
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:05,880
Picking Q because it's coding is the classic

161
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:06,600
mistake.

162
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:10,440
So when a question gives you a mutation,

163
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:11,640
ask two things.

164
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:13,400
Where is it in the body?

165
00:07:13,880 --> 00:07:17,480
Somatic affects only this organism, germ line can

166
00:07:17,520 --> 00:07:18,600
be inherited.

167
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:20,640
Where is it in the DNA?

168
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:23,640
In coding DNA, check the codons.

169
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:27,480
In non-coding DNA, ask whether that stretch controls

170
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:28,520
the gene.

171
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,720
Next, mutation practice questions.

172
00:07:32,934 --> 00:07:34,373
Here are the key notes.

173
00:07:34,614 --> 00:07:36,533
Pause the video if you need more time

174
00:07:36,614 --> 00:07:37,413
to copy them.
